{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786797096,"nonce":"Ve+c5mGZFtRVWS1yibIzzGK74uk=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABM4W0SQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/104","origin":"github"},"title":"fem.stats: linear-solver iteration counts, and step counts for custom_root-wrapped drivers","message":"## What\n\n`fem.stats` (landed 2026-08-15) reports the solve mode, slots, dispatch wall time, and the nonlinear drivers' outcome from their eager convergence check. Two observability gaps were scoped out and deserve tracking:\n\n1. **Linear iteration counts.** The `jno.solve` Krylov solvers return `x` only (the `LinearSolver` contract), and the `jax.scipy` wrappers' `info` is empty upstream. The own implementations (`krylov.py` fgmres/minres) carry iteration masks internally — surfacing a count means either widening the return contract (invasive: every composer consumes `x`) or a host-side slot written after eager solves, mirroring `LAST_NEWTON_STATS`.\n\n2. **Newton step counts for the traced-loop drivers.** `newton_direct` runs its forward `while_loop` outside `custom_root` and reports `steps`; `newton_krylov` and `staggered` iterate *inside* `custom_root`, where the count is a tracer, so they report `None`. Restructuring them to the forward-then-custom_root shape (as `newton_direct` and `staggered(direct=True)` already do) would make steps reportable everywhere — and is anyway the shape that lets the eager convergence guard see the loop's own counter.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nSolver observability was one of the three recorded gaps that stalled the rigid-plastic rolling project; the first slice landed but \"how many Krylov iterations did each Newton step cost\" — the number that tells you whether a preconditioner works — is still invisible.","files":null}]}